                        "review": "The Great Wobo Escape side-scrolling casual game with light stealth elements, its a game that feels aimed at a younger gamer. \n\nYou can find a about 15 minutes of gameplay with commentary here:\n[url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VxU5XyAetf4] https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VxU5XyAetf4 [/url] \n\nYou play as Wobo - a newly made robot on a remote mining station, a station controlled by robots. Your goal is to escape the station, as Wobo soon learns that staying will result in his destruction. The story is light, but there are 2 cut scenes that try to explain why Wobo and the station exists. \n\nMovement is left and right on the controller or keyboard, with a jump, an interact button and a sneak. Game mechanics are clearly explained by billboards when something new is encountered. It is not an overly technical game though, which lends it towards a younger gamer or someone who has no (or very little) experience with side scrolling or platform gaming.\n\nThe game involves no overt violence, when Wobo is captured or falls from a height, his arms, legs and head pop off and the game reloads from the last save point. This is another thing that I feel makes it a good game for a younger gamer, there was nothing in the game that would make me not want to let my own children have a turn.\n\nThere are 3 difficulty levels, an Easy level when you can use the MANY save points littered throughout a stage. A Hardcore setting that puts you back to the start of a stage if you die and a Time Attack mode where you can try to beat a defined counter per stage. All stages have the optional mission of saving other robots - \"Woblings\" - which provide a points bonus to your score. \n\nGame length will vary depending on your own abilities, but on the hardcore mode it took me about 2 hours to complete the 8 stages. The price is about right with the current discount and am happy to recommend it, I feel a younger gamer will get a lot of value from this title.",
